# RFP Hub client — local environment
#
# Copy this to `packages/frontend/.env.local`, which Next reads automatically and which is
# gitignored:
#
#   cp .env-example .env.local
#   pnpm --filter @the-rfp-hub/frontend dev
#
# ONE VARIABLE. There used to be two: the API's origin, and a third-party auth application id that
# had to be a DIFFERENT application per environment or a test login in development became a real
# account in production's directory. Sessions are now issued by the API itself, so the API's origin
# is the only thing this client needs to be told — and that entire class of misconfiguration is
# gone with the second variable.
#
# IT IS `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, WHICH MEANS TWO THINGS.
#
#   1. It is INLINED INTO THE BUNDLE AT BUILD TIME. Changing it on a running host does nothing;
#      the deployment has to be rebuilt. The frontend says so on screen when it is missing,
#      because this is the single most common way to lose an afternoon here.
#   2. It is PUBLIC. Anyone who loads the page can read it. It is not a secret — an API origin is
#      an identifier — and NOTHING SECRET MAY EVER BE ADDED WITH THIS PREFIX. This package holds no
#      server-side credential at all: every authenticated request is made by the browser with the
#      signed-in user's own session token.

# Where the API lives. No trailing slash needed (one is trimmed).
#
# This origin does three jobs: it is what `/v1` is called on, it is where Better-Auth is mounted
# (`/api/auth`), and it is written into the page's Content-Security-Policy `connect-src` — so the
# browser may talk to this API and to nothing else. That is why a deployment pointing at a
# different API must be rebuilt rather than reconfigured.
#
# The API must list this frontend's own origin in its TRUSTED_ORIGINS, or the browser's preflight
# for the sign-in calls is refused.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3004
